  "overview": {
    "claims": [
      {
        "attribution": "United States",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "cc44c5520d4086436d545e1c2c845157ebe7babeb0bc319052a0f735cda368d0",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-cc44c5520d40-0000-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 4,
              "start": 4
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "The United States argued that there is no cause of action under the Tucker Act because no violation of any act of Congress or executive regulation exists."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "United States",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "cc44c5520d4086436d545e1c2c845157ebe7babeb0bc319052a0f735cda368d0",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-cc44c5520d40-0000-0003"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 4,
              "start": 4
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to United States, the United States alleged that the Secretary of the Interior had private conversations with representatives of Peabody Coal to discourage approval of a $20 rate."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Respondent (Paul E. Frye)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "cc44c5520d4086436d545e1c2c845157ebe7babeb0bc319052a0f735cda368d0",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-cc44c5520d40-0001-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 27,
              "start": 27
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Respondent (Paul E. Frye), the Navajo Nation, through respondent Paul E. Frye, argued that violations of trust duties by the federal government give rise to a claim for money damages in the Court of Federal Claims, citing the Mitchell II principle."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Petitioner (Navajo Nation)",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "cc44c5520d4086436d545e1c2c845157ebe7babeb0bc319052a0f735cda368d0",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-cc44c5520d40-0002-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 40,
              "start": 40
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Petitioner (Navajo Nation), the Navajo Nation alleged that a decision was forthcoming that would hurt them and asked the Court to stop it, attributing responsibility to the Secretary."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Court",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "cc44c5520d4086436d545e1c2c845157ebe7babeb0bc319052a0f735cda368d0",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-cc44c5520d40-0002-0002",
              "ev-cc44c5520d40-0003-0000",
              "ev-cc44c5520d40-0003-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 55,
              "start": 53
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "Court records show that the tribe learned from Washington that they were requested to return to negotiations, and Secretary Hodel preferred negotiation over a final decision on the merits of the appeal."
      }
    ],
